{"id":"cluster-administration-swap-memory-management","corpus":"k8s","title":"Swap memory management","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/swap-memory-management/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Swap memory management"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes can be configured to use swap memory on a node, allowing the kernel to free up physical memory by swapping out pages to backing storage. This is useful for multiple use-cases. For example, nodes running workloads that can benefit from using swap, such as those that have large memory footprints but only access a portion of that memory at any given time. It also helps prevent Pods from being terminated during memory pressure spikes, shields nodes from system-level memory spikes that might compromise its stability, allows for more flexible memory management on the node, and much more."},{"kind":"p","text":"To learn about configuring swap in your cluster, read Configuring swap memory on Kubernetes nodes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Operating system support"},{"kind":"li","text":"Linux nodes support swap; you need to configure each node to enable it. By default, the kubelet will not start on a Linux node that has swap enabled."},{"kind":"li","text":"Windows nodes require swap space. By default, the kubelet does not start on a Windows node that has swap disabled."},{"kind":"h2","text":"How does it work?"},{"kind":"p","text":"There are a number of possible ways that one could envision swap use on a node. If kubelet is already running on a node, it would need to be restarted after swap is provisioned in order to identify it."},{"kind":"p","text":"When kubelet starts on a node in which swap is provisioned and available (with the failSwapOn: false configuration), kubelet will:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Be able to start on this swap-enabled node."},{"kind":"li","text":"Direct the Container Runtime Interface (CRI) implementation, often referred to as the container runtime, to allocate zero swap memory to Kubernetes workloads by default."},{"kind":"p","text":"Swap configuration on a node is exposed to a cluster admin via the memorySwap in the KubeletConfiguration. As a cluster administrator, you can specify the node's behaviour in the presence of swap memory by setting memorySwap.swapBehavior."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Swap behaviors"},{"kind":"p","text":"You need to pick a swap behavior to use. Different nodes in your cluster can use different swap behaviors."},{"kind":"p","text":"The swap behaviors you can choose for Linux nodes are:"},{"kind":"p","text":"NoSwap (default)"},{"kind":"p","text":"Workloads running as Pods on this node do not and cannot use swap."},{"kind":"p","text":"LimitedSwap"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes workloads can utilize swap memory."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you choose the NoSwap behavior, and you configure the kubelet to tolerate swap space (failSwapOn: false), then your workloads don't use any swap."},{"kind":"p","text":"However, processes outside of Kubernetes-managed containers, such as systemd services (and even the kubelet itself!) can utilize swap."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can read configuring swap memory on Kubernetes nodes to learn about enabling swap for your cluster."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Container runtime integration"},{"kind":"p","text":"The kubelet uses the container runtime API, and directs the container runtime to apply specific configuration (for example, in the cgroup v2 case, memory.swap.max) in a manner that will enable the desired swap configuration for a container. For runtimes that use control groups, or cgroups, the container runtime is then responsible for writing these settings to the container-level cgroup."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Observability for swap use"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Node and container level metric statistics"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubelet now collects node and container level metric statistics, which can be accessed at the /metrics/resource (which is used mainly by monitoring tools like Prometheus) and /stats/summary (which is used mainly by Autoscalers) kubelet HTTP endpoints. This allows clients who can directly request the kubelet to monitor swap usage and remaining swap memory when using LimitedSwap. Additionally, a machine_swap_bytes metric has been added to cadvisor to show the total physical swap capacity of the machine. See this page for more info."},{"kind":"p","text":"For example, these /metrics/resource are supported:"},{"kind":"li","text":"node_swap_usage_bytes: Current swap usage of the node in bytes."},{"kind":"li","text":"container_swap_usage_bytes: Current amount of the container swap usage in bytes."},{"kind":"li","text":"container_swap_limit_bytes: Current amount of the container swap limit in bytes."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Using kubectl top --show-swap"},{"kind":"p","text":"Querying metrics is valuable, but somewhat cumbersome, as these metrics are designed to be used by software rather than humans. In order to consume this data in a more user-friendly way, the kubectl top command has been extended to support swap metrics, using the --show-swap flag."},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to receive information about swap usage on nodes, kubectl top nodes --show-swap can be used:"},{"kind":"p","text":"This will result in an output similar to:"},{"kind":"p","text":"In order to receive information about swap usage by pods, kubectl top pods --show-swap can be used:"}],"lemmas":{"swaps":["swap","swaps"],"memories":["memory","memories"],"managements":["management","managements"],"kubernetes":["kubernetes","kubernete"],"configured":["configured","configure"],"configureds":["configureds","configured"],"configures":["configures","configure"],"allowing":["allowing","allow"],"allowings":["allowings","allowing"],"allowed":["allowed","allow"],"kernels":["kernel","kernels"],"frees":["free","frees"],"freeing":["freeing","free"]